WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.770 --> 00:00:12.960
Today's featured presenters are

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:18.600
Professor Mark Zoback and a research associate Arjun Kohli.

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.310
Mark Zoback is the Benjamin Page Professor of Geophysics and the

00:00:23.310 --> 00:00:28.005
director of the Stanford Natural Gas Initiative at Stanford University.

00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:33.225
He co-directs the Stanford Center for induced and triggers seismicity.

00:00:33.225 --> 00:00:37.815
Dr. Zoback conducts research on in situ stress,

00:00:37.815 --> 00:00:41.360
fault mechanics, and reservoir geomechanics.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.300
He is the author of a textbook entitled

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:50.285
Reservoirs Geomechanics published in 2007 by Cambridge University Press.

00:00:50.285 --> 00:00:56.380
The author, co-author of 400 technical papers and holder of five patents.

00:00:56.380 --> 00:00:59.400
His online course, Reservoirs Geomechanics,

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:03.930
has been completed by over 10,000 students around the world.

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:08.090
In addition, Dr. Zoback has received a number of awards and

00:01:08.090 --> 00:01:12.710
honors and he serves on the National Academy of Energy Committee

00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:17.030
investigating the Deepwater Horizon accident and the Secretary of

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:22.405
Energy's Committee on Shale Gas Development and Environmental Protection.

00:01:22.405 --> 00:01:26.630
Dr. Arjun Kohli is a research scientist and

00:01:26.630 --> 00:01:31.565
lecturer in the Department of Geophysics at Stanford University.

00:01:31.565 --> 00:01:36.319
He conducts research and earthquake physics to understand

00:01:36.319 --> 00:01:42.670
the behavior of plate boundary fault and the occurrence of induced seismicity.

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:49.165
He co-developed two massively open online geomechanics courses with Dr. Zoback,

00:01:49.165 --> 00:01:54.575
and coordinated interactive assignments designed for students with diverse backgrounds,

00:01:54.575 --> 00:01:59.355
ranging from high school to industry professionals.

00:01:59.355 --> 00:02:02.030
And once again my name is Cecilia Canales,

00:02:02.030 --> 00:02:06.110
I'm a program manager of the Academic Programs team at the Stanford Center for

00:02:06.110 --> 00:02:12.045
Professional Development and I will be moderating today's webinar and the Q&amp;A session.

00:02:12.045 --> 00:02:17.400
Now, I'd like to turn the floor over to Mark and Arjun.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.670
Well, good morning. I'm Mark-

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:25.470
I'm Arjun.

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.940
And what we want to tell you about today is

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:34.445
the course and give you a sense for the material that we'll be covering.

00:02:34.445 --> 00:02:39.530
I think everyone is probably generally aware of the fact that in the last 10 years in

00:02:39.530 --> 00:02:42.335
the United States and Canada there has been

00:02:42.335 --> 00:02:45.860
truly a revolution in the oil and gas industry,

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:50.030
which has to do with producing natural gas and

00:02:50.030 --> 00:02:54.665
producing oil from what are called Unconventional Reservoirs.

00:02:54.665 --> 00:02:59.750
Reservoirs which had been known forever but have extremely low permeability.

00:02:59.750 --> 00:03:04.330
And all this has happened in just the past 10 years.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.464
As it turns out,

00:03:06.464 --> 00:03:09.820
the new abundance of natural gases has

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.880
changed the energy landscape in the United States quite markedly,

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:17.059
not only is a vast,

00:03:17.059 --> 00:03:19.339
supply of natural gas now available,

00:03:19.339 --> 00:03:21.710
the switching of

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:25.010
electrical power generation away from coal and towards

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:28.865
natural gas has resulted in a dramatic decrease in CO2,

00:03:28.865 --> 00:03:33.300
the emissions which is, continuing to, go down all the time

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:36.795
that the process of fuel switching is still underway.

00:03:36.795 --> 00:03:41.130
So, in addition to providing a cheap and abundant energy source

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:46.780
that's having very market beneficial effects on the environment.

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:52.835
This slide, is from ConocoPhillips and was developed

00:03:52.835 --> 00:03:57.620
to show the fact that just three or four of these major plays,

00:03:57.620 --> 00:03:59.120
the Bakken in North Dakota,

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:00.905
the Marcellus in the Northeast,

00:04:00.905 --> 00:04:03.455
the Eagle Ford in the Permian Basin.

00:04:03.455 --> 00:04:06.200
These four plays will produce

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:09.830
so much hydrocarbon that they're comparable to major

00:04:09.830 --> 00:04:10.580
discoveries.

00:04:10.580 --> 00:04:13.490
They're on a scale of major oil fields,

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:15.605
oil and gas fields around the world.

00:04:15.605 --> 00:04:20.330
And to date about 200,000 wells have been drilled

00:04:20.330 --> 00:04:22.528
in the United States and Canada.

00:04:22.528 --> 00:04:26.360
There's been a lot of activity in this last decade.

00:04:26.360 --> 00:04:32.150
And so while the total amount of hydrocarbons produced in

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.730
from these and other reservoirs has been quite

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.655
enormous as the previous slide showed,

00:04:37.655 --> 00:04:41.075
there are still some major issues that need to be addressed.

00:04:41.075 --> 00:04:43.160
For example, the recovery factor,

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:47.120
the fraction of hydrocarbons that are successfully

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:51.440
recovered from dry gas reservoirs, like the Barnett Shale in

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:54.770
the Dallas Fort Worth area for example is only about

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:55.925
25 percent.

00:04:55.925 --> 00:04:59.875
In other words, 75 percent of the gas is left behind.

00:04:59.875 --> 00:05:03.150
When we're talking about tight oil or

00:05:03.150 --> 00:05:07.025
oil being produced from very low permeability reservoirs,

00:05:07.025 --> 00:05:10.670
we're talking about recovery factors that range between

00:05:10.670 --> 00:05:15.705
about two and 10 percent with about 5 percent being a reasonable average.

00:05:15.705 --> 00:05:21.320
So, while a great deal of hydrocarbons is being produced and it's had,

00:05:21.320 --> 00:05:29.140
this dramatic effect on the US economy and industry, consumers, and so on,

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:30.830
the fact of the matter is we were leaving

00:05:30.830 --> 00:05:34.560
the great majority of these hydrocarbons behind.

00:05:35.270 --> 00:05:38.370
One issue affecting the

00:05:38.370 --> 00:05:42.230
recovery of hydrocarbons is illustrated in this slide on the

00:05:42.230 --> 00:05:43.070
left what we're

00:05:43.070 --> 00:05:47.540
showing is production as a function of time from the

00:05:47.540 --> 00:05:48.530
Eagle Ford,

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:51.690
from the Bakken,

00:05:51.690 --> 00:05:55.680
from the Barnett and from the Marcellus.

00:05:55.680 --> 00:05:57.975
Two are oil and two are liquid.

00:05:57.975 --> 00:06:04.430
The horizontal scale is time and what you can see is that the production

00:06:04.430 --> 00:06:07.520
from each of these reservoirs decreases very

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:11.120
markedly over the period of the first two or three years.

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:15.470
The different colored lines represent two-year cohorts.

00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:20.420
So, we can actually trace how well we're doing as a function

00:06:20.420 --> 00:06:23.585
of time during the lifetime of the given play.

00:06:23.585 --> 00:06:27.200
On the right side, we're actually looking at the cumulative production,

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:28.790
the average production per well.

00:06:28.790 --> 00:06:32.690
By the way, each of these lines is supported by a minimum of

00:06:32.690 --> 00:06:37.230
400 wells and as many as several thousand wells.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:39.154
So, these are robust averages.

00:06:39.154 --> 00:06:43.260
What you can see is that as a function of time,

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.200
as the play develops say for example in the yield for

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.120
between 2009 and 2016,

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:52.850
technology is improving and we're recovering

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:56.975
more hydrocarbons but we're only recovering about 50 percent more,

00:06:56.975 --> 00:07:00.765
currently, than we were in the early years,

00:07:00.765 --> 00:07:02.895
the same is true in the Barnett,

00:07:02.895 --> 00:07:04.620
the same is true in the Marcellus.

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:07.545
In the case of the

00:07:07.545 --> 00:07:10.905
excuse me, in the Barnett and the Bakken.

00:07:10.905 --> 00:07:13.520
In the Marcellus, we're actually producing

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:20.965
about twice as much hydrocarbon as we were before. What this means is that

00:07:20.965 --> 00:07:28.620
we're really actually not doing all that well because over time,

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:33.030
what's happened is the wells have gotten longer, the horizontal wells,

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:38.780
the number of hydraulic fractures, the size of the hydraulic fractures has-had-

00:07:38.780 --> 00:07:40.040
have all increased.

00:07:40.040 --> 00:07:44.475
So, the level of effort has increased markedly.

00:07:44.475 --> 00:07:49.390
And yet the total cumulative production has, increased modestly.

00:07:49.390 --> 00:07:51.940
So, the economics are quite complicated.

00:07:51.940 --> 00:07:53.350
Obviously, the resource price,

00:07:53.350 --> 00:07:55.945
oil and gas prices fluctuate, that's important.

00:07:55.945 --> 00:07:59.875
Operational efficiency has improved remarkably,

00:07:59.875 --> 00:08:05.005
and that's one thing that is very important component to all this.

00:08:05.005 --> 00:08:07.960
But the level of effort, has also improved,

00:08:07.960 --> 00:08:12.040
and we've not really made a lot of progress in improving

00:08:12.040 --> 00:08:17.500
the overall recovery factors that were discussed in the previous slide, and so,

00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:23.145
while one could describe these major fields as discoveries,

00:08:23.145 --> 00:08:26.280
what's really discovered is not the fact that

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:29.460
there's oil or gas in a particular formation,

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.070
what's been discovered is the technology that enables

00:08:33.070 --> 00:08:37.760
us to extract the hydrocarbons in an economic way.

00:08:37.950 --> 00:08:48.415
And when we look to the future, there's essentially an unlimited potential for more drilling to occur.

00:08:48.415 --> 00:08:54.190
The numbers associated with six of the major plays in the United States

00:08:54.190 --> 00:08:57.910
come from the Bureau of Economic Geology at the University of Texas.

00:08:57.910 --> 00:09:02.890
They've made these estimates based on drilling only into

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:04.825
already known reservoirs,

00:09:04.825 --> 00:09:10.345
at places that are already approved for drilling.

00:09:10.345 --> 00:09:16.306
In other words, we're not drilling in areas that are

00:09:16.306 --> 00:09:19.150
in population centers, and national parks,

00:09:19.150 --> 00:09:24.025
or it's just kind of a continuation of activity,

00:09:24.025 --> 00:09:27.250
and if you leave out,

00:09:27.250 --> 00:09:29.245
the majority of the places,

00:09:29.245 --> 00:09:32.995
and just consider five of the top six,

00:09:32.995 --> 00:09:36.880
there's as many as 600,000 potential new wells,

00:09:36.880 --> 00:09:39.805
if you include the Permian Basin of West Texas,

00:09:39.805 --> 00:09:42.685
there is a million possible wells there,

00:09:42.685 --> 00:09:45.100
because there's hydrocarbons at multiple levels,

00:09:45.100 --> 00:09:48.235
it's called stack pay that could be exploited.

00:09:48.235 --> 00:09:51.280
These numbers really are- they really don't mean anything.

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:54.460
Well, you know, we have to decarbonize our energy system over

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:55.779
the next several decades,

00:09:55.779 --> 00:09:58.225
these wells will never be drilled.

00:09:58.225 --> 00:10:01.060
But the way to think about this is that there's

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:03.970
an unlimited amount of drilling that could occur,

00:10:03.970 --> 00:10:09.850
and therefore addressing these issues of recovery factors,

00:10:09.850 --> 00:10:11.770
optimizing the wells,

00:10:11.770 --> 00:10:17.140
reducing the environmental impacts associated with drilling and exploitation,

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:20.665
and using, say the natural gas resource,

00:10:20.665 --> 00:10:24.880
really need to be addressed as early as possible,

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.355
because despite you know,

00:10:27.355 --> 00:10:31.390
a couple of a 100,000 wells being drilled overall in the US and Canada,

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:36.410
there's a lot more activity that's going to be going on in the future.

00:10:36.480 --> 00:10:41.170
Now, the basic technology that's used to extract

00:10:41.170 --> 00:10:47.875
these hydrocarbons is horizontal drilling and multistage hydraulic fracturing.

00:10:47.875 --> 00:10:52.150
Again the characteristic of these unconventional reservoirs is that the

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:57.400
permeability, the ability for fluid to flow through the cracks and pores in the rock

00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:59.320
is extremely low.

00:10:59.320 --> 00:11:03.625
In fact, it's a million times lower than a conventional reservoir.

00:11:03.625 --> 00:11:07.420
So, what was discovered about 10 years ago

00:11:07.420 --> 00:11:10.720
in the Barnett Shell in the Dallas Fort Worth area,

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:13.315
is that if you were to drill horizontally,

00:11:13.315 --> 00:11:16.615
and you can see this schematically on the left

00:11:16.615 --> 00:11:17.859
and then again,

00:11:17.859 --> 00:11:21.775
in on the upper right, you drill horizontally,

00:11:21.775 --> 00:11:26.500
and then you hydraulically fracture the well for what's called the toe,

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:28.810
the furthest away from the vertical section of the well,

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:30.190
and you hydraulically fracture it,

00:11:30.190 --> 00:11:35.530
successively coming back toward what's called the appeal of the well,

00:11:35.530 --> 00:11:39.040
and the hydraulic fracturing is done in stages,

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:44.200
certain distance along the well is isolated and pressurized at a time.

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:47.950
And that's illustrated in the in the schematic diagram.

00:11:47.950 --> 00:11:51.310
Now, what's also shown there are a lot of little tick marks,

00:11:51.310 --> 00:11:54.130
and those tick marks represent microearthquakes.

00:11:54.130 --> 00:11:56.455
Very small little earthquakes,

00:11:56.455 --> 00:11:58.705
they are about magnitude minus two,

00:11:58.705 --> 00:12:04.840
what that really means is that slip is occurring on a patch that's about a meter in size,

00:12:04.840 --> 00:12:06.910
and slips about a tenth of a millimeter.

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:10.180
The energy release from each of these microearthquakes is

00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:13.495
about the same as a gallon of milk falling off the kitchen counter.

00:12:13.495 --> 00:12:15.535
So, they're very small events,

00:12:15.535 --> 00:12:20.050
but the cumulative effect of slip on all these faults,

00:12:20.050 --> 00:12:24.670
puts a lot of permeable fractures and faults into communication

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:28.840
with the very impermeable reservoir rock allowing for

00:12:28.840 --> 00:12:34.045
the hydrocarbons to flow at a rate that's economically feasible.

00:12:34.045 --> 00:12:36.790
So, it's a complex process,

00:12:36.790 --> 00:12:42.220
horizontal drilling, hydraulic fracturing with what's called slick water,

00:12:42.220 --> 00:12:46.450
the fracturing is basically done with water so that the fluid leaks out of the

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:52.450
hydrofrac, hydrofracs, and stimulates slip on these many small faults.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:53.890
And examples of that,

00:12:53.890 --> 00:12:57.985
those micro-seismic events can be seen in the lower part of the diagram.

00:12:57.985 --> 00:12:59.440
And in the lower right,

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:03.940
we illustrate a kind of a computer model of how all of this happens as we try

00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:09.260
to better understand the various processes that are underway.

00:13:09.840 --> 00:13:14.035
In this course, we address

00:13:14.035 --> 00:13:17.680
all of these things from the perspective of first principles,

00:13:17.680 --> 00:13:20.680
and these first principles may come from laboratory studies,

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:22.945
or they may come from theories,

00:13:22.945 --> 00:13:26.470
and then we implement these ideas and

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:30.850
illustrate their importance through integrated case studies.

00:13:30.850 --> 00:13:35.215
And these case studies will be presented throughout the course.

00:13:35.215 --> 00:13:39.055
So, for example, in the upper right of this diagram,

00:13:39.055 --> 00:13:42.400
what you see is a well trajectory in the Marcellus Shell,

00:13:42.400 --> 00:13:44.665
and toward the heel of a well,

00:13:44.665 --> 00:13:47.365
off to the left, and near the vertical section,

00:13:47.365 --> 00:13:52.600
the hydraulic fractures are vertical and little micro seismic events can be seen,

00:13:52.600 --> 00:13:55.810
which are both in the interval being hydraulically fractured,

00:13:55.810 --> 00:13:57.520
and just above it.

00:13:57.520 --> 00:14:00.220
Obviously, we'd like to keep the stimulation,

00:14:00.220 --> 00:14:02.695
in the zone with hydrocarbons.

00:14:02.695 --> 00:14:04.960
As that well was drilled however,

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:10.585
it dips down into another formation shown in light green color,

00:14:10.585 --> 00:14:12.040
and in that area,

00:14:12.040 --> 00:14:15.250
the hydraulic fractures are actually horizontal.

00:14:15.250 --> 00:14:18.760
This change in sort of a stress state,

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:21.250
it's the stress state that controls the orientation

00:14:21.250 --> 00:14:25.330
of the hydraulic fracture and the pressure needed to, propagate it

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:31.000
is a result of the viscal plastic nature of rock deformation,

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.130
and we're gonna talk about what that means.

00:14:33.130 --> 00:14:36.940
Basically it means that the rocks deform as a function of time,

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:40.840
and we're gonna illustrate that by talking about the laboratory measurements,

00:14:40.840 --> 00:14:45.564
we will develop some simple theories that explain how this creep occurs,

00:14:45.564 --> 00:14:48.190
and as shown in the cartoon in the lower left,

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:52.105
how it affects the magnitude of stress in the various layers,

00:14:52.105 --> 00:14:57.145
which ultimately control how hydraulic fractures propagate up and down,

00:14:57.145 --> 00:14:58.840
why they are vertical in some cases,

00:14:58.840 --> 00:15:01.510
why they're horizontal in others.

00:15:01.510 --> 00:15:05.290
Similarly, in the diagram in the lower right,

00:15:05.290 --> 00:15:10.645
those are some computer calculations of pressure change,

00:15:10.645 --> 00:15:13.060
and the three different

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:14.590
windows show different

00:15:14.590 --> 00:15:16.885
assumed initial values

00:15:16.885 --> 00:15:20.515
whether the initial pore pressure is low,

00:15:20.515 --> 00:15:21.850
sort of medium, or high,

00:15:21.850 --> 00:15:29.275
the stress magnitudes vary accordingly, and what you can see is there's a well

00:15:29.275 --> 00:15:33.250
in the center that goes sort of up and down on the diagram,

00:15:33.250 --> 00:15:35.725
hydrofracs that go from left to right,

00:15:35.725 --> 00:15:38.359
and then the different other lines are

00:15:38.359 --> 00:15:42.275
these little faults upon which they are sharing and they're more permeable.

00:15:42.275 --> 00:15:45.500
And what you can see is that the depletion,

00:15:45.500 --> 00:15:47.240
as you put all this into production,

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:51.430
the pressure changes in sort of these halos surrounding

00:15:51.430 --> 00:15:56.569
the permeable fractures whether they're the hydrofracs or they stimulate shear fractures.

00:15:56.569 --> 00:15:59.420
This results from what's seen on the left,

00:15:59.420 --> 00:16:03.140
and that's the extremely low permeability of these formations.

00:16:03.140 --> 00:16:07.165
Again, if you are familiar with permeability,

00:16:07.165 --> 00:16:10.245
a typical reservoir might have a permeability,

00:16:10.245 --> 00:16:16.155
a conventional reservoir, of one or two hundred millidarcys.

00:16:16.155 --> 00:16:18.499
In the case of an unconventional reservoir,

00:16:18.499 --> 00:16:21.135
the permeability would be

00:16:21.135 --> 00:16:24.690
a 100 nanodarcys or even lower.

00:16:24.690 --> 00:16:27.255
So, because the permeability is so low,

00:16:27.255 --> 00:16:30.464
the hydrocarbons can't travel very far,

00:16:30.464 --> 00:16:36.060
and the hydrocarbons that are produced are produced from the

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:37.720
area only immediately

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:41.070
adjacent to the more permeable fracture planes such as

00:16:41.070 --> 00:16:44.945
the hydrofracks and such as that the shear fractures.

00:16:44.945 --> 00:16:47.225
And this affects not only the ultimate recovery,

00:16:47.225 --> 00:16:50.405
why the recovery is so low, for example,

00:16:50.405 --> 00:16:54.840
but also how depletion and in- stress changes occur in nature

00:16:54.840 --> 00:16:56.040
and how, well

00:16:56.040 --> 00:16:58.800
what this tends to mean for subsequent drilling,

00:16:58.800 --> 00:17:03.820
infill drilling, interaction between different wells, and so on.

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:06.790
Thanks Mark,

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:10.040
the course is gonna be divided basically into three sections.

00:17:10.040 --> 00:17:12.660
In the first part we'll look at the physical properties of

00:17:12.660 --> 00:17:16.170
the rocks that actually comprise these unconventional reservoirs.

00:17:16.170 --> 00:17:18.005
In the second part of the course,

00:17:18.005 --> 00:17:21.090
we'll consider the processes of stimulating production from

00:17:21.090 --> 00:17:22.520
unconventional reservoirs,

00:17:22.520 --> 00:17:25.530
and go over the basics of horizontal drilling and

00:17:25.530 --> 00:17:31.440
hydraulic fracturing and consider slip on induced slip on sheer faults.

00:17:31.440 --> 00:17:34.115
And in the third part of the course we'll consider

00:17:34.115 --> 00:17:38.760
environmental impacts and talk a little bit more about induced seismicity.

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:40.710
So, as I said,

00:17:40.710 --> 00:17:44.795
the first part we'll focus mostly on the physical properties of the rocks,

00:17:44.795 --> 00:17:48.170
and to start with, we'll focus on the composition,

00:17:48.170 --> 00:17:53.045
fabric, elastic properties, and anisotropy which provides a good basis for thinking

00:17:53.045 --> 00:17:58.930
about the rock frame and starting to think about mechanical properties and flow.

00:17:59.790 --> 00:18:03.785
So, unconventional reservoir rocks span a wide range of

00:18:03.785 --> 00:18:07.585
lithologies from siliceous to clay-rich to calcareous.

00:18:07.585 --> 00:18:12.030
And as can be seen from the core samples that are around this plot,

00:18:12.030 --> 00:18:15.310
even within tens of meters of each other,

00:18:15.310 --> 00:18:19.170
you can have significantly different compositions, and, of course,

00:18:19.170 --> 00:18:20.890
that can play out,

00:18:20.890 --> 00:18:23.915
in differences in micro structures between the samples.

00:18:23.915 --> 00:18:26.500
And so, we'll talk a little bit about how each of

00:18:26.500 --> 00:18:32.210
the mineral and organic matter components are arranged in these rocks and build up

00:18:32.210 --> 00:18:35.375
the structure of them in terms of their anisotropy and

00:18:35.375 --> 00:18:39.785
the relationship between different compositional groups for example,

00:18:39.785 --> 00:18:42.820
organic content and clay content.

00:18:43.010 --> 00:18:47.380
And we'll focus on looking at the elastic properties of the rock.

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:49.840
So, on the left is a summary of

00:18:49.840 --> 00:18:54.320
different laboratory studies of elastic properties from the Haynesville,

00:18:54.320 --> 00:18:56.360
Eagle Ford, and Barnett reservoirs.

00:18:56.360 --> 00:18:59.490
And we'll use this to start looking at differences in how

00:18:59.490 --> 00:19:03.000
the sample orientation plays out in differences between

00:19:03.000 --> 00:19:06.780
the elastic properties and also relate that to

00:19:06.780 --> 00:19:09.160
the microstructural properties that we discussed

00:19:09.160 --> 00:19:12.455
previously as well as the range of compositions.

00:19:12.455 --> 00:19:15.990
And through that we'll basically build up a framework

00:19:15.990 --> 00:19:20.475
for understanding the anisotropy at various scales in the rock.

00:19:20.475 --> 00:19:24.090
We'll move on by considering mechanical properties in

00:19:24.090 --> 00:19:26.310
context of strength and ductility.

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:28.595
So, we'll focus on rock strength,

00:19:28.595 --> 00:19:30.890
how the rocks fail under pressure,

00:19:30.890 --> 00:19:33.960
and relate that to both the composition and

00:19:33.960 --> 00:19:35.825
the microstructure as well as

00:19:35.825 --> 00:19:39.485
the elastic properties that we discussed in the first section.

00:19:39.485 --> 00:19:44.620
As Mark mentioned, these rocks are also shown to deform visco-plastically,

00:19:44.620 --> 00:19:48.300
so under constant stress deform consistently as a

00:19:48.300 --> 00:19:50.410
function of time and that actually

00:19:50.410 --> 00:19:53.025
varies very considerably between

00:19:53.025 --> 00:19:56.525
the different compositions and microstructures of the rocks.

00:19:56.525 --> 00:20:00.500
So, we'll focus on laboratory measurements to try and understand

00:20:00.500 --> 00:20:05.540
a simple theory to describe the time-dependent deformation of these rocks,

00:20:05.540 --> 00:20:09.640
as well as how that varies between different reservoirs and

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:12.050
within each reservoir group.

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:15.780
We'll use this to actually understand how the state of

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:18.975
stress might vary between different layers of the rock,

00:20:18.975 --> 00:20:21.970
so on the left is a diagram showing

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:26.650
different lithologies and the evolution of the frack radiant as

00:20:26.650 --> 00:20:29.500
the state of stress changes with different amounts of

00:20:29.500 --> 00:20:33.335
different viscoplastic deformation within each of the groups.

00:20:33.335 --> 00:20:37.530
On the right is a Mohr circle which we'll get into more detail within the course,

00:20:37.530 --> 00:20:40.550
but the basic point to understand is that

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:45.150
increasing viscoplastic deformation decreases stress and anisotropy,

00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:51.130
so decreases, the distance between S H max and S H min as can be seen there.

00:20:51.130 --> 00:20:54.720
And differences between different layers are gonna determine

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:59.180
how fracks are able to propagate and grow vertically.

00:20:59.210 --> 00:21:04.065
We'll finish the discussion of the mechanical properties in the course by focusing on

00:21:04.065 --> 00:21:08.815
frictional properties and so here we'll focus on

00:21:08.815 --> 00:21:14.200
the physics of what determines whether you get actually micro-earthquakes or

00:21:14.200 --> 00:21:17.060
slower stable slip during induced,

00:21:17.060 --> 00:21:19.470
during shear stimulation of faults.

00:21:19.470 --> 00:21:22.815
So, on the left is a schematic diagram showing

00:21:22.815 --> 00:21:27.110
two different frictional responses that are observed in laboratory experiments.

00:21:27.110 --> 00:21:30.930
The first on the top shows that increasing the speed actually

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:35.520
increases the resistance to slip which would tend to produce stable sliding,

00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:40.110
whereas on the bottom increasing the speed of sliding actually decreases

00:21:40.110 --> 00:21:42.740
the resistance to slip which would tend to produce

00:21:42.740 --> 00:21:45.915
instability and has the potential for micro-earthquakes.

00:21:45.915 --> 00:21:49.475
As can be seen on the right in the laboratory data,

00:21:49.475 --> 00:21:52.310
the frictional properties of

00:21:52.310 --> 00:21:55.185
these rocks are highly dependent on composition.

00:21:55.185 --> 00:21:57.170
And so on the top,

00:21:57.170 --> 00:21:59.940
looking at clay-rich rocks we can see that as

00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:03.025
the friction decreases with increasing clay content,

00:22:03.025 --> 00:22:05.670
adding more weak minerals into the structure,

00:22:05.670 --> 00:22:10.870
the frictional stability actually changes from samples that are

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:13.960
high clay being stable siding to samples that are

00:22:13.960 --> 00:22:17.260
low clay being unstable siding in our group are,

00:22:17.260 --> 00:22:20.490
potentially hosting microearthquakes.

00:22:20.490 --> 00:22:23.745
For calcareous rocks, there's

00:22:23.745 --> 00:22:27.365
quite a complex pattern which we'll get into more, and again,

00:22:27.365 --> 00:22:32.140
relate back to the microstructures which we discussed in the previous section.

00:22:32.150 --> 00:22:35.585
We'll continue our discussion of the rock properties by

00:22:35.585 --> 00:22:38.350
shifting over to talking about flow and

00:22:38.350 --> 00:22:44.330
considering the pore networks and pore fluids that are within these rocks.

00:22:44.330 --> 00:22:47.040
As Mark alluded to before,

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:50.010
these rocks span a vast range of scales because of

00:22:50.010 --> 00:22:52.180
the small pores and

00:22:52.180 --> 00:22:55.769
low-permeability that are characteristic of unconventional reservoirs.

00:22:55.769 --> 00:22:59.860
On the left is a summary of

00:22:59.860 --> 00:23:04.720
these rocks at different scales both describing the features moving from

00:23:04.720 --> 00:23:09.545
fractures, to micro fractures to small pores in

00:23:09.545 --> 00:23:13.020
organic matter and as well as the characterization tools

00:23:13.020 --> 00:23:15.090
that are used to.

00:23:15.090 --> 00:23:17.690
determine porosity at each of these scales.

00:23:17.690 --> 00:23:21.490
So, we'll spend a lot of time actually going over each of these methods in

00:23:21.490 --> 00:23:25.530
detail talking about their applications and using

00:23:25.530 --> 00:23:30.600
that to basically build up a scale-wise picture of the rocks going all the way from

00:23:30.600 --> 00:23:36.475
nanometer scale pores in organic matter to meter scale fractures,

00:23:36.475 --> 00:23:40.415
which are actually hosting the micro-earthquakes that were described before.

00:23:40.415 --> 00:23:43.505
And in that we'll also focus on,

00:23:43.505 --> 00:23:50.140
basically, what tools can be used at what scales to characterize porosity.

00:23:51.540 --> 00:23:57.440
We'll end the discussion of physical properties by focusing on flow and sorption.

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:04.075
So, on the left is a schematic diagram of several flow mechanisms.

00:24:04.075 --> 00:24:08.770
The first viscous flow represents Darcy flow that you might expect in

00:24:08.770 --> 00:24:12.670
conventional high permeability reservoirs in which

00:24:12.670 --> 00:24:15.640
the gas molecules are interacting much

00:24:15.640 --> 00:24:18.835
more with each other than actually the walls of the pore.

00:24:18.835 --> 00:24:23.245
But since we have such small pore sizes in these unconventional rocks,

00:24:23.245 --> 00:24:25.960
as you get down to much smaller sizes,

00:24:25.960 --> 00:24:28.750
you actually have more interactions between

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:33.400
the molecules and the walls than the molecules and with each other,

00:24:33.400 --> 00:24:38.440
and this introduces different physics of flow namely diffusion.

00:24:38.440 --> 00:24:40.690
So, we'll talk a little bit about

00:24:40.690 --> 00:24:43.780
the conditions under which those arise,

00:24:43.780 --> 00:24:45.820
their impacts on permeability,

00:24:45.820 --> 00:24:50.000
and their pressure dependence on the context of depletion.

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:52.570
As Mark mentioned before,

00:24:52.570 --> 00:24:54.400
these rocks are much,

00:24:54.400 --> 00:24:58.375
much less permeable than conventional rocks almost a million times as permeable.

00:24:58.375 --> 00:25:01.440
The permeability here is, nanodarcys where you might

00:25:01.440 --> 00:25:05.175
expect something like hundreds of millidarcys for conventional rocks,

00:25:05.175 --> 00:25:08.430
and again because of the small pores that we

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:12.285
observe in these rocks introduces different physics of flow.

00:25:12.285 --> 00:25:15.600
So, we'll focus a little bit on both how

00:25:15.600 --> 00:25:19.350
the permeability changes as a function of stress as these reservoirs

00:25:19.350 --> 00:25:23.680
are depleted and how that change as a function of stress changes

00:25:23.680 --> 00:25:29.470
the relative proportions of diffusive versus Darcy flow that we observe in the reservoir,

00:25:29.470 --> 00:25:33.950
and consider how that impacts production that over time.

00:25:34.260 --> 00:25:38.170
So, now I hand it back over to Mark for part two.

00:25:38.170 --> 00:25:40.480
In the second part of the course,

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:44.560
we're going to scale up to the reservoir and the first topic

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:48.640
we're going to look at is the state of stress,

00:25:48.640 --> 00:25:49.960
the initial pore pressure,

00:25:49.960 --> 00:25:54.760
and the fractures and faults that are in these reservoir rocks, and so,

00:25:54.760 --> 00:25:57.474
we're going to present a series of new

00:25:57.474 --> 00:26:00.145
stress maps that we've made each of the

00:26:00.145 --> 00:26:02.560
lines in this map.

00:26:02.560 --> 00:26:06.070
This is the Permian Basin in West Texas and Southeastern Mexico.

00:26:06.070 --> 00:26:09.670
Shows the direction of maximum horizontal compression.

00:26:09.670 --> 00:26:14.980
In the right side of the Permian Basin and the east side of

00:26:14.980 --> 00:26:18.085
the Permian Basin in what's called the Midland basin, we see

00:26:18.085 --> 00:26:23.199
a very uniform direction of maximum horizontal stress east northeast,

00:26:23.199 --> 00:26:26.860
and the color indicates a relatively constant

00:26:26.860 --> 00:26:28.420
relative stress state.

00:26:28.420 --> 00:26:29.740
It's a reflection

00:26:29.740 --> 00:26:31.720
of the magnitudes

00:26:31.720 --> 00:26:33.190
of the in situ stresses.

00:26:33.190 --> 00:26:37.540
As we go further west and wind up in the western part of the

00:26:37.540 --> 00:26:40.645
the Permian Basin which is called the Delaware basin,

00:26:40.645 --> 00:26:43.615
what we see is a much less compressive stress state,

00:26:43.615 --> 00:26:47.140
the blue color indicates lower stress magnitudes and a stress state

00:26:47.140 --> 00:26:52.330
that's highly variable spatial in the northern part of the Delaware,

00:26:52.330 --> 00:26:55.855
the maximum horizontal stresses is north south,

00:26:55.855 --> 00:26:58.420
that would mean that if you are drilling a well

00:26:58.420 --> 00:27:01.120
you'd probably want to drill that well east-west.

00:27:01.120 --> 00:27:04.585
But as you go from north to south into Delaware basin

00:27:04.585 --> 00:27:07.135
the stress orientation changes

00:27:07.135 --> 00:27:10.210
quite markedly meaning that the optimal direction for

00:27:10.210 --> 00:27:14.200
drilling also changes as a function of wearing war.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:17.140
So, you wanna know something about the stress magnitude and you

00:27:17.140 --> 00:27:20.140
wanna know something about the stress orientation

00:27:20.140 --> 00:27:23.860
to drill the wells in an optimal orientation.

00:27:23.860 --> 00:27:26.350
If we look at the eastern United States,

00:27:26.350 --> 00:27:31.390
the area where the Marcellus and Utica formations are being exploited

00:27:31.390 --> 00:27:34.675
the stress orientation is relatively constant

00:27:34.675 --> 00:27:37.570
as can be seen by the straight black lines,

00:27:37.570 --> 00:27:39.835
but the colors are warmer.

00:27:39.835 --> 00:27:44.470
Warm colors mean higher stress magnitudes and we see a gradient going from

00:27:44.470 --> 00:27:47.340
the western part of this map to the eastern part of

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:51.880
the map and can lead to even horizontal hydraulic fractures being

00:27:51.880 --> 00:27:55.195
generated in some areas and in some formations.

00:27:55.195 --> 00:27:58.780
So, this is all you know basic fundamental information

00:27:58.780 --> 00:28:00.760
that has to be known in order to

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:02.980
understand what's happening in situ as you

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:07.460
pressurize the rock and create hydraulic fractures.

00:28:07.530 --> 00:28:10.120
In the next part of the course,

00:28:10.120 --> 00:28:13.150
we'll get into some of the operational issues on how

00:28:13.150 --> 00:28:15.865
hydraulic fracturing is being done.

00:28:15.865 --> 00:28:18.820
The most common technique is illustrated on the left.

00:28:18.820 --> 00:28:20.695
It's called plugin curve the well.

00:28:20.695 --> 00:28:23.200
The horizontal section of the well which

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:27.070
typically today ranges between 5 and 10,000 feet

00:28:27.070 --> 00:28:29.170
in length

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:32.680
is that there's us a steel casing put in,

00:28:32.680 --> 00:28:34.150
it's cemented into place,

00:28:34.150 --> 00:28:36.460
and then that casing is perforated,

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:41.290
successively from the the toe of the hill off to the right in this case,

00:28:41.290 --> 00:28:43.210
and working weight back,

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:44.814
toward the hill.

00:28:44.814 --> 00:28:49.920
Over time the number of hydraulic fractures per well has increased.

00:28:49.920 --> 00:28:54.330
It's not uncommon for 30 or 40 different frack stages that

00:28:54.330 --> 00:28:58.840
that means 30 or 40 in- intervals which were separately pressurized

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:01.300
to extend hydraulic fractures.

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:03.235
There are other, some other techniques

00:29:03.235 --> 00:29:04.840
illustrated on the right,

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:06.550
it's called sliding slip.

00:29:06.550 --> 00:29:08.155
We'll get into this in the course,

00:29:08.155 --> 00:29:10.810
but basically the well does not have to be casted and

00:29:10.810 --> 00:29:13.390
cemented ahead of time in order to

00:29:13.390 --> 00:29:16.659
carry out multiple hydraulic fractures.

00:29:16.659 --> 00:29:19.270
This is data from Canada which is

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:23.830
illustrating the micro seismic events coming from two wells.

00:29:23.830 --> 00:29:28.840
The well that's oriented northwest-southeast

00:29:28.840 --> 00:29:30.880
was hydraulically fractured

00:29:30.880 --> 00:29:35.080
sort of a viscous gel was used as a hydraulic fracturing fluid,

00:29:35.080 --> 00:29:38.830
and you can see the micro-seismic events associated,

00:29:38.830 --> 00:29:43.990
with the success of hydraulic fractures each color represents a different stage,

00:29:43.990 --> 00:29:46.045
a different position that was pressurized.

00:29:46.045 --> 00:29:48.820
The well that's oriented north-south

00:29:48.820 --> 00:29:54.460
is not in an ideal orientation

00:29:54.460 --> 00:29:57.310
but instead of using a gel, they use,

00:29:57.310 --> 00:30:00.490
water to hydraulically fracture, this well.

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:02.740
You can see that the hydraulic fractures are

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:05.710
propagating in the expected direction in both cases,

00:30:05.710 --> 00:30:07.705
sort of northeast southwest,

00:30:07.705 --> 00:30:10.060
but there are many many more

00:30:10.060 --> 00:30:13.030
micro seismic events for the north-south well because

00:30:13.030 --> 00:30:17.395
a lower viscosity fluid was being used for hydraulic fracturing.

00:30:17.395 --> 00:30:20.710
That means that the pressure could bleed out of

00:30:20.710 --> 00:30:26.590
the hydraulic fractures and stimulate slip on, on more faults.

00:30:26.590 --> 00:30:29.380
So, while the orientation was an ideal,

00:30:29.380 --> 00:30:33.655
the fluid that was being used was better and therefore the,

00:30:33.655 --> 00:30:35.800
the production was,

00:30:35.800 --> 00:30:39.940
was reflecting the enhanced stimulation.

00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:46.000
Of course, this is a great dataset that illustrates

00:30:46.000 --> 00:30:49.540
the importance of micro seismic data and then kind of

00:30:49.540 --> 00:30:54.399
indicating what's happening in the subsurface during the stimulation process,

00:30:54.399 --> 00:30:57.820
and we'll talk about sort of reservoir seismology.

00:30:57.820 --> 00:30:59.980
How do we, you know, measure this?

00:30:59.980 --> 00:31:01.555
How do we record these events?

00:31:01.555 --> 00:31:04.990
How do we interpret what each of the little dots means?

00:31:04.990 --> 00:31:10.950
And how do we use that information to draw a more integrated picture of the,

00:31:10.950 --> 00:31:14.020
the process of stimulation.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:15.880
as I said earlier,

00:31:15.880 --> 00:31:19.705
the process of stimulation is in fact.

00:31:19.705 --> 00:31:25.325
Trying to accomplish two very important things at the same time.

00:31:25.325 --> 00:31:29.145
One is the creation of hydraulic fractures which are these blue planes.

00:31:29.145 --> 00:31:31.150
So, this is a sort of a well,

00:31:31.150 --> 00:31:33.075
going, through this

00:31:33.075 --> 00:31:38.960
model from left to right and for perforated zones are being,

00:31:38.960 --> 00:31:41.520
pressurized in one stage.

00:31:41.520 --> 00:31:44.490
The hydraulic fractures are propagating perpendicular to

00:31:44.490 --> 00:31:50.345
the least horizontal stress in the earth and in-between the hydraulic fractures,

00:31:50.345 --> 00:31:55.040
you see these thousands of small events in which shear was,

00:31:55.040 --> 00:31:59.870
induced by the pressure that leaks out of the hydraulic fractures and as I said earlier,

00:31:59.870 --> 00:32:04.720
the cumulative production depends on both of these processes occurring,

00:32:04.720 --> 00:32:09.290
the hydraulic fracturing and the distributed shear on small folds.

00:32:09.290 --> 00:32:11.155
This slide is

00:32:11.155 --> 00:32:12.735
maybe a good place

00:32:12.735 --> 00:32:15.140
to just pause for a second and

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:19.495
allow us to make the point of how the rock properties,

00:32:19.495 --> 00:32:22.680
which vary because the load path as you can see

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:24.860
intersecting different formations,

00:32:24.860 --> 00:32:27.145
affect the state of stress and how the,

00:32:27.145 --> 00:32:32.640
the state of stress affects the stimulation of shear on pre-existing faults,

00:32:32.640 --> 00:32:36.335
and how when the well is down in

00:32:36.335 --> 00:32:38.050
the green formation there,

00:32:38.050 --> 00:32:40.780
we're getting horizontal hydraulic fractures and when

00:32:40.780 --> 00:32:44.105
the well is in the upper of the unit shaded in gray,

00:32:44.105 --> 00:32:46.360
we're getting vertical hydraulic fractures.

00:32:46.360 --> 00:32:48.640
And so, the rock properties matter,

00:32:48.640 --> 00:32:51.450
and the rock properties map into variations of stress.

00:32:51.450 --> 00:32:54.450
The variations of stress then control how

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:58.750
the stimulation process is occurring and the stimulation process,

00:32:58.750 --> 00:33:02.200
that's occurring tells you how you should be drilling your wells.

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:07.100
What landing zone you should stay in and so on.

00:33:07.100 --> 00:33:16.900
Okay. Another aspect of optimization of hydraulic fracturing has to do with,

00:33:16.900 --> 00:33:20.980
using some advanced models that can actually

00:33:20.980 --> 00:33:25.840
simulate the propagation of hydraulic fractures and this case

00:33:25.840 --> 00:33:28.030
we were modeling one stage with

00:33:28.030 --> 00:33:31.880
four sets of perforations that's illustrated at the top,

00:33:31.880 --> 00:33:34.650
and then the cross-sectional diagram shows how

00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:38.140
the hydraulic fracture is propagating.

00:33:38.140 --> 00:33:41.660
The unusual thing here is that the,

00:33:41.660 --> 00:33:46.090
hydrocarbons and the well is placed in the formation "D"

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:48.915
that's the thin formation for

00:33:48.915 --> 00:33:50.930
the bottom of this stack.

00:33:50.930 --> 00:33:55.075
We've got some idea of how the stress varies from layer to

00:33:55.075 --> 00:33:59.620
layer and because of the way in which hydraulic fracturing was occurring,

00:33:59.620 --> 00:34:02.995
the hydraulic fracture propagation is principally up,

00:34:02.995 --> 00:34:06.840
and out of the zone that contains hydrocarbons, and

00:34:06.840 --> 00:34:09.780
we vary a different experimental

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:13.300
of procedures for example, the flow rate,

00:34:13.300 --> 00:34:14.315
the amount of proppant,

00:34:14.315 --> 00:34:16.840
the diameter of the perforations,

00:34:16.840 --> 00:34:20.195
and we examine how that affects the hydraulic fracturing growth.

00:34:20.195 --> 00:34:26.070
What this modeling has revealed in fact is that there's

00:34:26.070 --> 00:34:29.960
too much propagation out of the zone of interest.

00:34:29.960 --> 00:34:34.470
The proppant which the proppant concentration is illustrated in red,

00:34:34.470 --> 00:34:39.005
how the proppant concentration is actually propping open the fracture and

00:34:39.005 --> 00:34:42.305
formations with relatively little hydrocarbons and so,

00:34:42.305 --> 00:34:47.070
one would then reexamine the operational procedures that are being

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:52.530
used because of the nature of how stress varies from layer, layer to layer.

00:34:52.530 --> 00:34:59.430
The stimulation process

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:04.800
is important in setting up how

00:35:04.800 --> 00:35:06.940
the well is going to be sort of

00:35:06.940 --> 00:35:10.459
depleting the formation around it once it goes into production,

00:35:10.459 --> 00:35:12.360
and that of course affects how

00:35:12.360 --> 00:35:17.324
subsequent wells are drilled or infill drilling is occurring.

00:35:17.324 --> 00:35:19.130
The example on the left,

00:35:19.130 --> 00:35:22.615
is a recently a published case study from,

00:35:22.615 --> 00:35:27.485
from Hess and what they are showing is that when wells,

00:35:27.485 --> 00:35:29.470
H2, and H3,

00:35:29.470 --> 00:35:30.990
and H4 were drilled,

00:35:30.990 --> 00:35:36.070
surrounding well H1, which had been in production for a number

00:35:36.070 --> 00:35:40.140
of years, the hydraulic fractures were actually propagating

00:35:40.140 --> 00:35:44.250
toward well H1 because the depletion,

00:35:44.250 --> 00:35:48.215
the lower- the decrease in pressure due to production affected the stresses,

00:35:48.215 --> 00:35:50.780
the stresses go down and therefore,

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:52.440
in the case of

00:35:52.440 --> 00:35:54.320
the diagram on

00:35:54.320 --> 00:35:58.560
in the upper left, when well H2 was being hydraulically fractured,

00:35:58.560 --> 00:36:01.660
what you can see is that the fractures propagate very

00:36:01.660 --> 00:36:05.315
strongly to the southwest toward H1,

00:36:05.315 --> 00:36:07.090
toward this depleted zone.

00:36:07.090 --> 00:36:08.805
Well, this is very problematic.

00:36:08.805 --> 00:36:14.650
Not are- not only are you not stimulating the area to the east of well H2,

00:36:14.650 --> 00:36:17.915
the majority of the hydraulic fracture propagation

00:36:17.915 --> 00:36:21.185
is going into an area that's already been depleted,

00:36:21.185 --> 00:36:22.920
during the production of H1.

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:26.576
On the right-hand side,

00:36:26.576 --> 00:36:30.090
the same thing happens when H4 was being stimulated.

00:36:30.090 --> 00:36:33.545
The hydraulic fractures propagate up toward H1 and again,

00:36:33.545 --> 00:36:38.070
you're not stimulating the area to the- to the west of H4

00:36:38.070 --> 00:36:43.340
and you are propagating the fracture into a zone that's already depleted.

00:36:43.340 --> 00:36:46.610
And so, this is a quite problematic and

00:36:46.610 --> 00:36:50.135
obviously, these wells are too close together and

00:36:50.135 --> 00:36:53.855
the infill drilling is not going to achieve its

00:36:53.855 --> 00:36:57.820
desired outcome because of the depletion surrounding H1.

00:36:57.820 --> 00:36:59.590
So, we're going to study this

00:36:59.590 --> 00:37:01.810
in terms of

00:37:01.810 --> 00:37:05.255
what happens, and how the pore pressure change of excess stress,

00:37:05.255 --> 00:37:08.020
and how the stress affects the

00:37:08.020 --> 00:37:13.560
hydraulic fracture propagation and what's likely to be the subsequent production.

00:37:13.560 --> 00:37:16.270
The figure on the right are

00:37:16.270 --> 00:37:20.230
some model calculations and what they're illustrating there is that

00:37:20.230 --> 00:37:23.580
the depletion can actually cause

00:37:23.580 --> 00:37:27.600
extraordinarily complex changes in the stress field.

00:37:27.600 --> 00:37:30.080
In the example that the

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:34.909
real case on the left the stress orientations don't change,

00:37:34.909 --> 00:37:37.980
the stress magnitudes do so the hydraulic fractures

00:37:37.980 --> 00:37:39.740
have the expected orientation,

00:37:39.740 --> 00:37:42.455
but they're pulled in toward the depleted zone.

00:37:42.455 --> 00:37:44.175
In the area on the right,

00:37:44.175 --> 00:37:46.790
the stress orientation changes

00:37:46.790 --> 00:37:48.965
are quite remarkable,

00:37:48.965 --> 00:37:51.360
and quite complicated and in fact,

00:37:51.360 --> 00:37:52.925
hydraulic fractures will almost have

00:37:52.925 --> 00:37:56.925
unpredictable orientations as they approach this depleted zone.

00:37:56.925 --> 00:37:59.120
Well you know,

00:37:59.120 --> 00:38:02.375
which is correct and which

00:38:02.375 --> 00:38:04.055
should we be expecting,

00:38:04.055 --> 00:38:07.050
and we go through this from a theoretical point of view

00:38:07.050 --> 00:38:10.270
in the diagram on the left is

00:38:10.270 --> 00:38:12.000
the figures I showed earlier,

00:38:12.000 --> 00:38:14.630
and it turns out that the

00:38:14.630 --> 00:38:20.440
way in which depletion occurs depends on the initial stress state,

00:38:20.440 --> 00:38:21.850
the initial pore pressures,

00:38:21.850 --> 00:38:25.855
the initial distribution of permeable fractures and faults,

00:38:25.855 --> 00:38:31.730
and then that maps into the kinds of stress changes that will affect

00:38:31.730 --> 00:38:33.905
wells that are drilled subsequently,

00:38:33.905 --> 00:38:38.350
and what controls something which is often called the

00:38:38.350 --> 00:38:39.770
frack hit phenomenon

00:38:39.770 --> 00:38:43.940
where a hydraulic fracture from a second well is,

00:38:43.940 --> 00:38:46.650
intersecting a pre-existing well.

00:38:46.650 --> 00:38:50.670
And so, we go through this again from the

00:38:50.670 --> 00:38:52.925
perspective of first principles, so

00:38:52.925 --> 00:38:56.260
it's easier to understand why a

00:38:56.260 --> 00:39:00.485
case like the one shown on the left occurred and under

00:39:00.485 --> 00:39:03.160
what circumstances it might be possible for

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:07.570
situations such as the one on the right to occur.

00:39:08.390 --> 00:39:12.010
The third part of the book

00:39:12.010 --> 00:39:17.615
addresses issues of environmental impacts and induced seismicity.

00:39:17.615 --> 00:39:23.470
The occurrence of environmental impacts of

00:39:23.470 --> 00:39:24.815
of shale gas and,

00:39:24.815 --> 00:39:26.775
and tight oil development

00:39:26.775 --> 00:39:28.595
is well-known and there's,

00:39:28.595 --> 00:39:31.035
a lot of concern in areas about

00:39:31.035 --> 00:39:32.985
the impacts of development.

00:39:32.985 --> 00:39:36.335
This figure sort of organizes some of those

00:39:36.335 --> 00:39:38.430
impacts community issues,

00:39:38.430 --> 00:39:41.580
atmospheric issues, land issues and water issues.

00:39:41.580 --> 00:39:44.709
You know, this is a large-scale industrial process

00:39:44.709 --> 00:39:47.980
and there are definitely impacts on the environment,

00:39:47.980 --> 00:39:54.655
impacts on the people and- but what's necessary to do and

00:39:54.655 --> 00:39:58.110
something on the order of 200,000 wells have been drilled,

00:39:58.110 --> 00:39:59.625
and there have been problems,

00:39:59.625 --> 00:40:04.325
but the great majority of operations are carried out safely and

00:40:04.325 --> 00:40:09.220
without significant impact, and you simply have to identify what the issues are,

00:40:09.220 --> 00:40:11.810
and take the steps to prevent

00:40:11.810 --> 00:40:15.050
the negative impacts of the development.

00:40:15.050 --> 00:40:17.305
Now, two things

00:40:17.305 --> 00:40:22.790
are kind of germane to the subject matter of the book.

00:40:22.790 --> 00:40:25.610
One, and the course

00:40:25.610 --> 00:40:26.815
excuse me,

00:40:26.815 --> 00:40:28.805
one is induced seismicity.

00:40:28.805 --> 00:40:33.665
How does the stimulation and production

00:40:33.665 --> 00:40:36.450
affect earthquakes and, we'll talk about that in

00:40:36.450 --> 00:40:40.985
just a moment, and the other is greenhouse gas emissions.

00:40:40.985 --> 00:40:43.950
Are there emissions associated with

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:47.885
the well construction and well development which might be very hard to control?

00:40:47.885 --> 00:40:50.370
And we address both of these issues

00:40:50.370 --> 00:40:53.470
in the course especially,

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:56.105
the issue of induced seismicity.

00:40:56.105 --> 00:40:58.880
The issue of induced seismicity really

00:40:58.880 --> 00:41:03.990
came to public attention around 2010.

00:41:03.990 --> 00:41:09.610
This figure shows the number of earthquakes occurring

00:41:09.610 --> 00:41:11.470
in the Central and Eastern United States.

00:41:11.470 --> 00:41:13.630
The area shown by the map,

00:41:13.630 --> 00:41:15.950
as a function of time.

00:41:15.950 --> 00:41:20.490
And prior to about, 2008-2009,

00:41:20.490 --> 00:41:22.935
the number of earthquakes was principally

00:41:22.935 --> 00:41:25.480
the same as it had been for decades and decades.

00:41:25.480 --> 00:41:30.275
Occasionally, earthquakes occur,

00:41:30.275 --> 00:41:32.260
within what we call an intraplate area,

00:41:32.260 --> 00:41:34.660
far away from plate boundaries where

00:41:34.660 --> 00:41:36.705
the really larger earthquakes

00:41:36.705 --> 00:41:39.745
on the San Andreas fault and other plate boundaries occur.

00:41:39.745 --> 00:41:41.405
This is a natural occurrence.

00:41:41.405 --> 00:41:45.285
So, those earthquakes shown in orange are largely effect of,

00:41:45.285 --> 00:41:47.680
of natural geologic processes,

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:50.525
but what can be seen here is that at

00:41:50.525 --> 00:41:53.015
the exactly the same time where

00:41:53.015 --> 00:41:57.110
horizontal drilling and multistage hydraulic fracturing were occurring,

00:41:57.110 --> 00:42:02.274
there was a tremendous increase in the number of earthquakes,

00:42:02.274 --> 00:42:06.400
and many of these earthquakes actually were occurring in Oklahoma.

00:42:06.890 --> 00:42:10.780
The reasons for these earthquakes

00:42:10.780 --> 00:42:12.325
are well understood

00:42:12.325 --> 00:42:16.210
and the reason the number of earthquakes is subsiding is because

00:42:16.210 --> 00:42:20.550
the appropriate steps were taken in Oklahoma to reduce their occurrence.

00:42:20.550 --> 00:42:25.275
And so, what we'll talk about is how hydraulic fracturing can induce earthquakes,

00:42:25.275 --> 00:42:28.230
how the injection of water that

00:42:28.230 --> 00:42:31.480
flows back after hydraulic fracturing can induce earthquakes,

00:42:31.480 --> 00:42:34.490
and how earthquakes can be induced by

00:42:34.490 --> 00:42:38.855
just the injection of water that's co-produced with oil over time.

00:42:38.855 --> 00:42:41.450
And it was that in fact that

00:42:41.450 --> 00:42:43.480
the third process that was responsible

00:42:43.480 --> 00:42:45.375
for the great majority of these earthquakes,

00:42:45.375 --> 00:42:47.665
and when the volume of wate

00:42:47.665 --> 00:42:49.490
being injected was decreased,

00:42:49.490 --> 00:42:55.320
you can see the, the, the significant decrease in the number of events.

00:42:55.390 --> 00:42:59.080
Arjun and I have been working on a book for

00:42:59.080 --> 00:43:00.690
the past year.

00:43:00.690 --> 00:43:04.405
This book will be available early in 2019.

00:43:04.405 --> 00:43:06.885
The course will be,

00:43:06.885 --> 00:43:09.945
will be following this book so the book will provide,

00:43:09.945 --> 00:43:12.410
you know, more detailed discussions of theories,

00:43:12.410 --> 00:43:15.495
references, development

00:43:15.495 --> 00:43:16.855
of mathematical

00:43:16.855 --> 00:43:23.095
principles and, theory in more detail.

00:43:23.095 --> 00:43:25.880
For the final word, we're going to

00:43:25.880 --> 00:43:27.870
look at this quote from

00:43:27.870 --> 00:43:29.605
Henry Clifton Sorby

00:43:29.605 --> 00:43:31.010
over a 100 years ago.

00:43:31.010 --> 00:43:34.220
I thought it was pretty interesting that

00:43:34.220 --> 00:43:37.650
actually 110 years ago

00:43:37.650 --> 00:43:41.700
people were kind of flummoxed by the complexity of these rocks,

00:43:41.700 --> 00:43:45.760
and it's still now in 2018

00:43:45.760 --> 00:43:49.135
we're taking all the tools to bear to sort of figure out

00:43:49.135 --> 00:43:51.395
both the physical properties and also

00:43:51.395 --> 00:43:55.375
the stimulation process and recovery factors of these reservoirs.

00:43:55.375 --> 00:43:59.610
So, this is definitely an ongoing area of study but

00:43:59.610 --> 00:44:03.505
the principles laid out in the book are sort of the first effort

00:44:03.505 --> 00:44:08.480
to really put these into practice and sort of connect the dots,

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:13.310
so we can provide kind of a first principles bounded view of

00:44:13.310 --> 00:44:17.245
what's going on in these reservoirs during stimulation and production.

00:44:17.245 --> 00:44:21.445
So, I think we're ready to answer some questions.

00:44:21.925 --> 00:44:24.460
Here are some of the best questions that we have for you.

00:44:24.460 --> 00:44:26.815
So, the first one we have is

00:44:26.815 --> 00:44:31.550
Generally,  how do rock properties vary as a function of clay contents?

00:44:31.550 --> 00:44:34.780
I'll take this one.

00:44:34.780 --> 00:44:37.170
This is really going to be something that

00:44:37.170 --> 00:44:40.590
we cover in various different aspects of the book.

00:44:40.590 --> 00:44:42.510
In the first section,

00:44:42.510 --> 00:44:45.265
we'll talk about how clay content affects

00:44:45.265 --> 00:44:48.120
anisotropy, and elastic properties of rocks.

00:44:48.120 --> 00:44:51.060
We'll move to thinking about how it

00:44:51.060 --> 00:44:54.890
affects actually the ductility of those rocks

00:44:54.890 --> 00:44:56.550
and their ultimate strength.

00:44:56.550 --> 00:45:00.060
We'll also focus again on the frictional properties and how

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:02.460
clay content can be very important in determining

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:04.340
whether stable slips is occurring,

00:45:04.340 --> 00:45:07.295
or whether micro-earthquakes have the potential to occur.

00:45:07.295 --> 00:45:10.945
Then we'll carry that further into thinking about how clay content

00:45:10.945 --> 00:45:14.745
actually affects the pore networks that are present inside these rocks,

00:45:14.745 --> 00:45:18.160
and how those pore networks impact the flow properties of the rocks.

00:45:18.160 --> 00:45:22.585
So, the effective clay content will really be a theme that we carry through

00:45:22.585 --> 00:45:25.070
the entire first part of the course.

00:45:25.070 --> 00:45:26.740
As well as the creep, and

00:45:26.740 --> 00:45:28.630
result of creep on changing the

00:45:28.630 --> 00:45:30.420
stress levels in the formation.

00:45:30.420 --> 00:45:34.075
It's a very good question and it's a very important part

00:45:34.075 --> 00:45:35.735
of what we'll be covering.

00:45:35.735 --> 00:45:37.690
Great. I have another question.

00:45:37.690 --> 00:45:44.405
how do small faults in the reservoir impact reservoir continuity?

00:45:44.405 --> 00:45:47.875
Real examples would be appreciated.

00:45:47.875 --> 00:45:49.910
We'll talk about that.

00:45:49.910 --> 00:45:52.250
The simulation I was showing

00:45:52.250 --> 00:45:54.025
is aimed at getting that in.

00:45:54.025 --> 00:45:55.310
One part of the course,

00:45:55.310 --> 00:45:57.770
we're going to compare

00:45:57.770 --> 00:46:01.015
what we

00:46:01.015 --> 00:46:04.630
infer about those small faults from an image log,

00:46:04.630 --> 00:46:06.775
a log geophysical measurements

00:46:06.775 --> 00:46:09.640
made in the horizontal wells where we can see the fractures,

00:46:09.640 --> 00:46:12.965
then we will compare that to

00:46:12.965 --> 00:46:16.325
what we infer about the fractures from the micro seismic events,

00:46:16.325 --> 00:46:19.900
and then we're actually going to then incorporate that into some models,

00:46:19.900 --> 00:46:21.350
that we look at

00:46:21.350 --> 00:46:23.840
how the drainage is affected.

00:46:23.840 --> 00:46:27.675
So, I think what the questioner

00:46:27.675 --> 00:46:29.310
is getting at,

00:46:29.310 --> 00:46:32.135
is really a very essential issue,

00:46:32.135 --> 00:46:33.925
and that is the distribution,

00:46:33.925 --> 00:46:35.725
the number of the orientation

00:46:35.725 --> 00:46:40.760
their spatial variability of preexisting fractures and faults is really

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:44.755
what sort of sets the stage for a successful development

00:46:44.755 --> 00:46:46.905
and we'll make that point a number of ways

00:46:46.905 --> 00:46:49.230
indicating it both from

00:46:49.230 --> 00:46:54.480
by the perspective of first principles to then using real data sets to illustrate

00:46:54.480 --> 00:46:58.240
those impacts of,

00:46:58.240 --> 00:46:59.955
those fractures and faults.

00:46:59.955 --> 00:47:03.204
Great. You have another one,

00:47:03.204 --> 00:47:05.694
related to the environment.

00:47:05.694 --> 00:47:12.920
I understand that some six percent of the cement casing leak.

00:47:12.920 --> 00:47:17.050
How likely is this to affect groundwater?

00:47:17.050 --> 00:47:21.655
There are scary videos of gas coming out of people's water taps,

00:47:21.655 --> 00:47:24.060
How likely are these problems,

00:47:24.060 --> 00:47:26.505
and how can they be avoided?

00:47:26.505 --> 00:47:29.485
Yeah, that's an excellent question and again,

00:47:29.485 --> 00:47:33.010
something we discuss in the book.

00:47:33.010 --> 00:47:36.970
What the public needs to understand concerning this issue,

00:47:36.970 --> 00:47:40.285
is despite the fact that there have been

00:47:40.285 --> 00:47:43.830
a couple of 100,000 wells drilled and literally,

00:47:43.830 --> 00:47:46.380
millions of hydraulic fracturing stages,

00:47:46.380 --> 00:47:48.690
the contamination that has occurred in

00:47:48.690 --> 00:47:52.495
some places is not the direct result of the hydraulic fracturing.

00:47:52.495 --> 00:47:55.390
And we talk about why that is.

00:47:55.390 --> 00:47:57.825
But the contamination does occur,

00:47:57.825 --> 00:48:01.665
and it occurs when the wells are not constructed properly.

00:48:01.665 --> 00:48:05.390
When you don't drill a well properly, when you don't case it properly,

00:48:05.390 --> 00:48:09.295
when the cement is not covering all of the annulus,

00:48:09.295 --> 00:48:11.215
the space between the

00:48:11.215 --> 00:48:12.835
outside of the hole

00:48:12.835 --> 00:48:14.095
and the steel casing,

00:48:14.095 --> 00:48:16.195
when that's not done properly,

00:48:16.195 --> 00:48:21.520
leakage can occur and there can be contamination.

00:48:21.520 --> 00:48:24.030
The three things

00:48:24.030 --> 00:48:26.240
to remember is well construction,

00:48:26.240 --> 00:48:28.080
well construction, and well construction.

00:48:28.080 --> 00:48:30.650
And we will talk about that

00:48:30.650 --> 00:48:34.320
in the class and we refer to other sources of information.

00:48:34.320 --> 00:48:36.609
This is not a course about well construction,

00:48:36.609 --> 00:48:39.905
but it's an important issue,

00:48:39.905 --> 00:48:43.295
and the hydraulic fracturing itself is not the

00:48:43.295 --> 00:48:47.695
source of contamination but contamination does occur.

00:48:47.695 --> 00:48:52.360
I'm gonna take one last question. These are very good questions.

00:48:52.360 --> 00:48:59.110
What magnitude are the earthquakes you discussed

00:48:59.110 --> 00:49:02.685
that are triggered by industrial activities?

00:49:02.685 --> 00:49:03.900
Are they dangerous?

00:49:03.900 --> 00:49:09.745
The micro-earthquakes I was referring to,

00:49:09.745 --> 00:49:12.835
throughout

00:49:12.835 --> 00:49:15.320
this webinar, are extremely small,

00:49:15.320 --> 00:49:18.265
they're about magnitude minus two which means

00:49:18.265 --> 00:49:20.195
slip on a very small fault,

00:49:20.195 --> 00:49:22.370
very little energy is released.

00:49:22.370 --> 00:49:26.740
But induced seismicity occasionally accompanies hydraulic fracturing.

00:49:26.740 --> 00:49:29.970
And that's when the pressurization of the well

00:49:29.970 --> 00:49:34.785
is affecting a larger fault that's present.

00:49:34.785 --> 00:49:37.180
So, we talk about how to identify these,

00:49:37.180 --> 00:49:41.735
why they should be avoided and in the very last part of the course,

00:49:41.735 --> 00:49:47.080
the very last topic we talk about is managing the risk of induced seismicity.

00:49:47.080 --> 00:49:51.430
What steps need to be taken to make sure that that hasn't happened.

00:49:51.430 --> 00:49:54.745
Just to put all this in perspective,

00:49:54.745 --> 00:49:56.390
the well,

00:49:56.390 --> 00:49:59.090
the typical magnitude for these thousands of events is,

00:49:59.090 --> 00:50:01.680
minus two.

00:50:01.680 --> 00:50:03.280
Events as large as

00:50:03.280 --> 00:50:05.665
magnitude four have occurred.

00:50:05.665 --> 00:50:09.700
Magnitude four earthquake is large enough to be felt

00:50:09.700 --> 00:50:11.360
by people at the surface,

00:50:11.360 --> 00:50:14.755
but not large enough to cause any damage.

00:50:14.755 --> 00:50:17.845
And no earthquake has been induced by

00:50:17.845 --> 00:50:21.810
hydraulic fracturing that's ever caused any damage or injury.

00:50:21.810 --> 00:50:24.759
So, it's a issue we want to avoid,

00:50:24.759 --> 00:50:26.980
but it's not something

00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:28.845
in millions of hydraulic fractures,

00:50:28.845 --> 00:50:32.695
that has yet to produce any significant impact.

00:50:32.695 --> 00:50:35.795
But you want to avoid

00:50:35.795 --> 00:50:39.015
triggering induced seismicity by hydraulic fracturing,

00:50:39.015 --> 00:50:40.860
by injection of flow back water,

00:50:40.860 --> 00:50:42.895
by injection of produce water

00:50:42.895 --> 00:50:44.075
you want to

00:50:44.075 --> 00:50:46.010
prevent that before it happens.

00:50:46.010 --> 00:50:49.310
Right. Thank you very much

